What is a backup?
A backup is an extra copy of data stored separately so that you can recover the original data if it is accidentally deleted, corrupted or lost.

Example:

Suppose you have this project folder:
linux-lab/
├── config/
│   └── app.conf
├── files/
│   └── employee.txt
└── logs/
    └── app.log


You create another copy:
linux-backup/
├── config/
│   └── app.conf
├── files/
│   └── employee.txt
└── logs/
    └── app.log


If employee.txt is deleted from the original folder, you can recover it from the backup.

Important: A backup is useful only if you can successfully restore it. Always test recovery.
2. Backup using rsync
rsync is a Linux command used to copy and synchronize files and directories.

Basic syntax:
rsync -av source/ destination/


Meaning:
Option	Purpose
-a	Archive mode; preserves important file attributes
-v	Verbose; displays what is copied

Understand the trailing slash
rsync -av source/ backup/


Copies the contents of source into backup.
rsync -av source backup/


Copies the source directory itself into backup.

This small difference is important when creating backups.
3. Practical Lab – Create and restore a backup
We will use only sample files inside your home directory. These commands will not modify your system files.

Step 1: Create a sample project
mkdir -p ~/linux-lab/backup-source/files
mkdir -p ~/linux-lab/backup


Step 2: Create a sample file
echo "Important employee data" > ~/linux-lab/backup-source/files/employee.txt


Check it:
cat ~/linux-lab/backup-source/files/employee.txt


Step 3: Create a backup
rsync -av ~/linux-lab/backup-source/ ~/linux-lab/backup/


Check the backup:
ls -R ~/linux-lab/backup


Step 4: Simulate accidental deletion

We will delete only the sample file we created:
rm ~/linux-lab/backup-source/files/employee.txt


Check that it is missing:
ls ~/linux-lab/backup-source/files/


Step 5: Restore the deleted file
rsync -av ~/linux-lab/backup/ ~/linux-lab/backup-source/


Verify:
cat ~/linux-lab/backup-source/files/employee.txt


Expected result
Important employee data


You have now performed a complete backup and recovery exercise.
4. Backup using tar
tar is used to combine files and directories into an archive. You can compress that archive using gzip.

Create a compressed backup:
tar -czvf linux-backup.tar.gz ~/linux-lab/backup-source


Options:
Option	Meaning
-c	Create archive
-z	Compress using gzip
-v	Display files
-f	Specify archive filename

List archive contents:
tar -tzvf linux-backup.tar.gz


Extract into a separate directory:
mkdir -p ~/linux-lab/restore-tar
tar -xzvf linux-backup.tar.gz -C ~/linux-lab/restore-tar


5. Linux system troubleshooting
Troubleshooting means finding the reason a system or application is not working correctly.

For example, imagine your Spark application is running very slowly. You should check CPU, memory, disk and network.
A. Check CPU and processes
top


Shows running processes and CPU/memory usage.

Press q to exit.
B. Check RAM
free -h


Shows total, used and available memory in human-readable units.
C. Check disk space
df -h


Shows filesystem disk usage.

Example problem: If a disk is 100% full, Spark or Hadoop may fail to write temporary files or logs.
D. Check disk performance statistics
iostat


This command is provided by the sysstat package. Check whether it is available:
command -v iostat


If it is missing and you want to practise it:
sudo apt install sysstat


E. Check kernel messages
sudo dmesg | tail


Displays recent kernel messages, which can help identify hardware, device or system-level problems.
F. Check network
These are commands from our previous networking lesson:
ip addr
ip route
ping -c 4 example.com
ss -tulpen


6. Troubleshooting sequence
Suppose your Spark application is not working. Don't randomly restart services. Investigate step by step.
Linux troubleshooting flow
1. Check the application
   
   Is Spark, Hive or Hadoop running? Check its logs and service status.
2. Check CPU
   
   Use top to identify high CPU usage.
3. Check memory
   
   Use free -h to inspect RAM availability.
4. Check disk
   
   Use df -h to identify full filesystems.
5. Check network
   
   Use ip addr, ip route and ping.
6. Find the cause and verify the fix
   
   Check logs again and test the application.

7. RAS, MTBF and MTTR
These are important terms in system administration.
Term	Full form	Meaning
RAS	Reliability, Availability, Serviceability	System reliability and ease of maintenance
MTBF	Mean Time Between Failures	Average operating time between failures
MTTR	Mean Time To Repair	Average time needed to repair a failure

Example:

Suppose a server works for 100 hours before a failure, and then takes 2 hours to repair.
- MTBF = 100 hours
- MTTR = 2 hours
In general, higher MTBF and lower MTTR are desirable.
